  "headline": "Amy Warden takes GW11 honours as Louis Grimoldby holds firm at the summit.",
  "summary": "amy warden’s Palmer Violets led the way with 51 points, while Louis Grimoldby matched Will Finney on 47 to keep Grim FC top on 737. Mark Horton remains Louis’s nearest challenger, 36 points back on 701, with olly barnes just 1 point behind Mark in a title race that is already tightly packed.",
  "report": "amy warden was the GW11 star, taking Manager of the Week with a clean 51-point haul for Palmer Violets. Pedro Porro’s 11 points and Szoboszlai’s 9 were both unique to Amy, giving the week-winning total some proper differential sparkle. Amy also climbed 3 places, matching Abi Speight’s 3-place rise after Abi’s 47 points.\n\nAt the top, Louis Grimoldby’s Grim FC remains the team to catch on 737 points. Louis posted 47, level with Will Finney, and maintained a 36-point lead over Mark Horton’s Vespa Mo Pedro on 701. Mark’s 31 was not enough to fend off the pressure completely: olly barnes is now only 1 point behind in third on 700, while Will sits another 6 back on 694 after his own 47-point week. David Bailey stays fifth on 681, but Tim Chodha is only 5 points behind.\n\nThe captaincy template was a tough watch. Haaland was handed the armband by 21 managers and returned 2 points, while David Tonks found the best captaincy call with Isak’s 5 points at double value. Jamie Mitchell, meanwhile, captained Calafiori for 1 point. Phil Johnson played Triple Captain on the way to 42 points, while Dan Birkinshaw’s Bench Boost also produced 42; 16 Triple Captains, 14 Bench Boosts and 18 Free Hits remain in reserve.\n\nWill Finney’s 47 was helped by Wirtz, whose 14 points were owned by only 2 managers: Will and Alan Eckett-Brown. Abi Speight had Hill’s 14-point return all to themself, while Paul Alexis landed Boscagli’s 7. George Foy and James Marshall were the only 2 managers with Barnes, who scored 7.\n\nThere was a painful bench story for olly barnes, who left 22 points sitting on the sidelines despite scoring 34 overall. At the other end, James Geary collected the wooden spoon with 19 points for Šeško Express and fell 3 places, alongside Chris Beeden’s 3-place drop after 27 and George Foy’s 2-place fall after 28. Ali Smith’s -8 hit turned a gross 28 into 20, extending a difficult spell that has produced 105 points across the last 3 gameweeks.\n\nGeorg Coakley remains the league’s hottest recent operator with 173 points across the last 3 gameweeks. For perspective, Mark Horton’s 116 in GW2 remains the season record, while GW11’s league average was 35.2 against a global average of 33 and a global high of 106.",
